welcome to the daily climate show coming  up we report from sweden where the  country is pursuing energy security  through an ambitious green energy  transition  well germany with the same goal  says some states should turn over two  per cent of their land to wind farms  and a warning on warming 200 000 homes  at risk along england's coastlines a  detailed look at the damage to come from  rising sea levels  [Music]  welcome to the daily climate show where  we examine the big solutions to the  climate crisis track the changes  happening to the planet and report on  the impact those changes are already  having  and we start today with news of a big  potential solution in part at least to a  problem many parts of the world are  grappling with  an energy crunch intensified by russia's  invasions mean any  many are looking to exploit more fossil  fuels as we reported on the programme  yesterday however sweden wants to pursue  a green and secure future with an  ambitious energy transition a  state-backed project to produce steel  without using fossil fuels a world first  has started supplying two car companies  in a bid to decarbonize heavy industry  our climate change and energy  correspondent hannah thomas peter is in  sweden for us at the hybrid steel pilot  site  i'm at the steel production facility in  sweden which has the potential to  transform the steel manufacturing  industry let me tell you a little bit  about it this is the basic ingredient in  making steel these are iron ore pellets  now in traditional steel making  you would melt these in a blast furnace  by burning coal that's why the steel  industry is such a polluting industry  accounts for about eight percent of  global carbon emissions but what they've  done here  is they've just decided to use hydrogen  made using electrolysis which is uh  fueled by clean green electricity  they're using that hydrogen to replace  the coal in the process and what happens  once they combine the iron ore pellets  with the hydrogen is they make iron  like this this is sponge iron  this sponge iron then gets  melted squashed into these iron  briquettes and they go off get alloys  added and it creates steel  now it sounds relatively simple but  swapping coal for hydrogen  essentially eliminates carbon emissions  and that is what is transformative  about this technology it's not widely  available yet starting to go into some  products in volvo for example  but it is more expensive and the big  question is will manufacturers and  ultimately consumers  be happy to pay that green premium to  start with  in order to get this technology off the  ground and in global circulation  well the green steel pilot is an example  of how a country can continue on the  quest for net zero while providing  domestic energy security and sweden  believes that the scheme can be an  example to others especially in europe  who remain heavily committed to fossil  fuels  the need for  for europe and the whole world to stop  using fossil fuels is immense that job  must continue at a rapid pace what this  crisis what this invasion aggression  that russia is doing against ukraine has  shown is that we need to even further  speed up this process and if there is  anything sweden can do is to lead by  example and show how we can we can  be less dependent on fossil in general  but specifically on russian energy  imports  let's get a bit more of today's climate  news now 10 eu countries led by denmark  have released a joint statement warning  of attempts by other member nations to  water down the region's green deal  austria spain and ireland were among the  countries who co-signed the letter ahead  of a crucial vote on the bloc's 2030  climate goals which are focused around  cutting emissions by 55  by the end of the decade from 1990  levels  german states may soon have to allocate  a minimum of two percent of its land to  wind farms under a plan approved by the  government today the proposed  legislation would have the 